      "dom_id": "question-134531",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.02)^{7}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{7}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{7} & =1+7x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 7\\cdot 6\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+7x+21{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{7}≈1+7x+21{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.02$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.02)^{7} & =(1+0.02)^{7} \\\\  & ≈1+7(0.02)+21(0.02)^{2} \\\\  & =1+7(0.02)+21(0.000\\,4) \\\\  & =1+0.14+0.008\\,4 \\\\  & =1.148\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.02)^{7}-1.148\\,4|}{(1.02)^{7}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.02%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
